## Proof

Let $f(x) = x + s(x)$. We seek the greatest $n$ for which there exists a sequence $a_1, \ldots, a_n$ with $f(a_i) = a_{i+1}$ and $a_n = 2008$.

**Key bound.** If $k < 2008$, then $k$ has at most 4 digits, so $s(k) \le 36$. Thus any predecessor of $m$ (a value $k$ with $f(k)=m$) satisfies $m - 36 \le k \le m - 1$.

We trace backward from 2008. For each $m$, we partition $[m-36, m-1]$ into decades and solve $s(k) = m - k$ within each. Writing $k = 1900 + 10p + t$ (with appropriate $p, t$), the digit sum becomes linear in $t$, reducing each case to a single linear equation.

